    <!-- Legal disclaimer (first-run sheet + Settings re-read) -->
    <string name="legal_title">Before you record</string>
    <string name="legal_intro">Jurisdictions fall into three tiers. Find yours and act accordingly. This is not legal advice.</string>

    <string name="legal_tier1_title">1. No notice required (one-party consent)</string>
    <string name="legal_tier1_summary">Ukraine, UK, US federal + ~38 states, Canada, Australia, Poland.</string>
    <string name="legal_tier1_body">A party to the conversation may record without notifying the other party. Your own consent is sufficient consent. Legal bases: the federal Wiretap Act, 18 U.S.C. § 2511 (US); RIPA 2000 (UK); Criminal Code § 184(2)(a) (Canada).</string>

    <string name="legal_tier2_title">2. Notice is enough (all-party + implied consent)</string>
    <string name="legal_tier2_summary">~11 US states: California, Illinois, Florida, Maryland, and others.</string>
    <string name="legal_tier2_body">Full list: CA (Penal Code § 632), FL, IL, MD, MA, MT, NV, NH, PA, WA, CT. Notify the other party at the start of the call — if they hear it and continue, that is implied consent and the recording is legal. The doctrine has been established by case law for decades. The same mechanism underlies commercial hotline disclaimers in the EU.</string>

    <string name="legal_tier3_title">3. Prohibited even with notice (explicit consent)</string>
    <string name="legal_tier3_summary">Germany, Austria, Belgium. Explicit consent is required — notice is legally insufficient.</string>
    <string name="legal_tier3_body">Germany — § 201 StGB (Vertraulichkeit des Wortes): up to 3 years\' imprisonment for recording “non-publicly spoken words” without explicit permission. German courts do not recognize implied consent in private conversations. Austria — § 120 StGB. Belgium — criminal law plus electronic communications law; written consent is often required. Do not use cally in these jurisdictions without an explicit “yes, I agree” from the other party.</string>

    <string name="legal_tech_title">cally and the beep for the other party</string>
    <string name="legal_tech_body">We technically cannot play a beep or announcement into the other party\'s ear — that is a system privilege of the Pixel Phone app, unavailable to third-party apps. A verbal notice at the start of the call works in tier 2 (implied consent). In tier 3 it is not enough — explicit consent is required.</string>

    <string name="legal_share">Recording ≠ distribution. The user is responsible for publishing a recording without the other party\'s consent — even in one-party jurisdictions.</string>
    <string name="legal_footer">Details and sources are in the project README.</string>
    <string name="legal_action_accept">Understood, continue</string>
    <string name="legal_action_close">Close</string>
    <string name="settings_calibration_done">Reset. The next call will run a fresh calibration.</string>

